//? Title: Hamming Distance: Positions, Bit Flips and Error Balls
//? Subtitle: Worked byte comparisons, a TikZ cube and finite error-correction geometry
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Fixed-width records or transmitted words may change in a few positions, and we want
to count those changes without shifting the alignment. Hamming distance counts unequal
symbols at corresponding positions. The meaning of a symbol matters: one changed byte
can contain several changed bits, and UTF-8 bytes are not Unicode characters. A TikZ
position diagram and binary cube introduce the geometry, followed by small word and
packed-bit examples, a strict edit-distance comparison, and finite codes with ambiguous
nearest words. Hamming-shell counts then explain error balls, correction limits and the
distribution of distances between uniform independent words. No real dataset is needed
for these exact finite constructions.
)

\toc

> The method

>> One differing position contributes one unit

Choose an alphabet and a common word length n. For two aligned words a and b,

$$
 d_H(a,b)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\mathbf1[a_i\ne b_i].
$$
\alttext(Hamming distance sums one for each unequal pair of symbols at the same position and zero for each equal pair.)

A coordinate contributes zero or one regardless of which two unequal symbols appear.
There is no insertion, deletion, padding or search for a better alignment. The following
TikZ diagram introduces both the positional count and its graph interpretation: on the
binary cube, an edge changes one bit and a shortest path changes each differing bit once.
The editable source is `diagrams/hamming_distance_concept.tikz`. Regeneration needs
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and the standalone class, plus dvisvgm. The shell block saves its
DVI, SVG and compile logs locally in `build/`; only the rendered image is referenced.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/hamming_distance_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/hamming_distance_concept.tikz > build/hamming_distance_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/hamming_distance_concept.svg build/hamming_distance_concept.dvi > build/hamming_distance_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/hamming_distance_concept.svg from the local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/hamming_distance_concept.svg from the local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/hamming_distance_concept.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Aligned symbols identify the changed positions; a binary cube represents each single-bit substitution as an edge. The red three-edge path is shortest between 000 and 111.)
\alttext(Two aligned seven-bit words differ at zero-based positions two and four. Beside them, a labelled three-dimensional binary cube highlights a path from 000 to 001 to 011 to 111, showing distance three.)

>> Why this is a metric, and what the algorithm costs

Nonnegativity, symmetry and the zero-distance condition follow coordinate by coordinate.
For the triangle inequality, if \(a_i\ne c_i\), at least one of \(a_i\ne b_i\)
or \(b_i\ne c_i\) must hold. Summing that indicator inequality gives
\(d_H(a,c)\le d_H(a,b)+d_H(b,c)\). The metric lives on words with one fixed length;
`hamming_distance()` throws `std::invalid_argument` on unequal byte lengths.
Both empty words are allowed and have distance zero.

The general string implementation compares all n bytes in one scan, using Θ(n) time
and O(1) auxiliary storage. This is optimal for a full positional comparison in the
worst case: a previously unread position might change the answer. Packed machine words
can exploit bit operations instead. For two unsigned 64-bit words,

$$
 d_{bit}(u,v)=\operatorname{popcount}(u\mathbin{\oplus}v).
$$
\alttext(XOR marks every unequal bit with a one, and population count counts those ones.)

`hamming_bit_distance()` implements this fixed-64-bit operation. It is O(1) for a
machine word, including its leading zero bits; processing an arbitrary packed bit array
would still require work proportional to its number of machine words.

>> Representation and alignment are part of the contract

`hamming_distance(std::string,std::string)` treats every byte, including embedded NUL,
as one symbol. It neither decodes UTF-8 nor normalizes Unicode. A printable ASCII binary
string has one byte per written bit, so its byte distance agrees with the corresponding
bit distance; an arbitrary packed byte is different. Text applications requiring Unicode
code points or grapheme clusters must choose and implement that representation first.
The byte comparison API does not silently substitute a different alphabet.

Any substitution-only script is also a legal Levenshtein edit script, hence for equal
lengths \(d_L(a,b)\le d_H(a,b)\). Insertions and deletions may allow cheaper realignment,
so the inequality need not be equality. The examples keep the same byte positions when
showing Hamming comparisons.

> Simple examples

>> Seven letters reveal the mismatching columns

`karolin` and `kathrin` disagree at zero-based positions 2, 3 and 4 and agree at
0, 1, 5 and 6. `hamming_comparison()` exposes the byte values, each mismatch indicator
and the cumulative count; the final cumulative count is the distance. The plot labels
printable ASCII directly and other byte values in hexadecimal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_words.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::hamming_comparison("karolin","kathrin");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::algorithms::plot_hamming_comparison("karolin","kathrin").size(900,340).save_svg("build/hamming_words.svg");
  std::print("Distance: {}. Empty pair: {}.\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::hamming_distance("karolin","kathrin"),dm::algorithms::hamming_distance("",""),t.to_md(0));
}
```
