>> Permitting realignment changes the answer

`abcdef` and `bcdefa` differ at all six fixed positions. Levenshtein distance is two:
delete the leading a, then insert it at the end. It cannot be one, because equal-length
distinct words at distance one must differ by a single substitution, and these differ at
six positions. Thus the edit upper bound is strict here. Whether realignment is desirable
depends on what a position represents in the application.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_alignment.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame result;result.add_column("metric",std::vector<std::string>{"Hamming: fixed alignment","Levenshtein: edits allowed"});result.add_column("distance",std::vector<double>{double(dm::algorithms::hamming_distance("abcdef","bcdefa")),double(dm::algorithms::levenshtein_distance("abcdef","bcdefa"))});
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::algorithms::plot_hamming_comparison("abcdef","bcdefa").title("A cyclic shift changes all six positions").size(900,340).save_svg("build/hamming_alignment.svg");std::print("{}\n",result.to_md(0));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| metric | distance |
| :--- | ---: |
| Hamming: fixed alignment | 6 |
| Levenshtein: edits allowed | 2 |

\image(figures/hamming_alignment.svg)
// result_end

\caption(A delete-and-insert script costs two edits while every fixed Hamming position differs. The two metrics encode different allowed changes.)
\alttext(The original six letters and their left cyclic shift disagree in all six aligned columns. The table reports Hamming distance six and Levenshtein distance two.)


>> Minimum code distance separates detection from correction

A finite codebook C is a set of distinct equal-width words. Its minimum distance is the
smallest separation between different valid codewords. If \(d_{min}\ge s+1\), every
pattern of one through s symbol changes leaves the codebook, so a receiver checking
membership detects it. Unique correction of every pattern of at most t changes requires
\(d_{min}\ge2t+1\): if two codewords were both within t of a received word, the
triangle inequality would put their separation at most 2t. Conversely, overlapping
radius-t balls arise when that bound fails. The universal radii are therefore

$$
 s_{detect}=d_{min}-1,\qquad t_{correct}=\left\lfloor\frac{d_{min}-1}{2}\right\rfloor.
$$
\alttext(Minimum code distance minus one is the guaranteed detection count; half that quantity, rounded down, is the guaranteed unique-correction count.)

For the three-bit repetition code {000,111}, minimum distance three gives detection of
up to two changed bits by membership checking and unique correction of one. A hard nearest
codeword decoder does not automatically signal every detectable error: starting from 000,
two flips can give 110, which is closer to the wrong word 111.

The even-parity code {000,011,101,110} has minimum distance two and no guaranteed
single-bit correction. The query 001 is tied at distance one from three codewords.
`hamming_nearest()` reports every tied minimum and does not silently pick a codeword.
No Hamming(7,4) encoder/decoder is needed for these finite geometric examples.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_codes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  const std::vector<std::string> repetition={"000","111"},parity={"000","011","101","110"};const auto distances=dm::algorithms::hamming_distance_matrix(parity);const auto nearest=dm::algorithms::hamming_nearest(repetition,"110");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> cells;for(std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i)cells.push_back(distances.row(i));auto matrix=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),query=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();matrix.image({0,1,2,3},{0,1,2,3},cells,dm::graphics::viridis_colors(3),0,2).title("Even-parity code: pairwise distances").x_label("codeword index: 000, 011, 101, 110").y_label("codeword index");
  query.bars({0,1},nearest.numeric_column("distance").values(),"distance from 110",{37,99,235},.5).title("Two flips can cause a wrong correction").x_label("repetition codeword: 0 = 000, 1 = 111").y_label("Hamming distance").y_limits(0,3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(matrix).add(query).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/hamming_codes.svg");
  std::print("Repetition code:\n\n{}\n\nEven-parity code:\n\n{}\n\nQuery 001 in the even-parity code:\n\n{}\n\nQuery 110 in the repetition code:\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::hamming_code_summary(repetition).to_md(0),dm::algorithms::hamming_code_summary(parity).to_md(0),dm::algorithms::hamming_nearest(parity,"001").to_md(0),nearest.to_md(0));
}
```
