> Checking the theory

>> Shell counts, error balls and uniform-word distances

There are \(\binom nk\) ways to choose k changed positions and \((q-1)^k\) ways to
replace them over an alphabet of size q. Therefore a radius-t ball around any fixed word
contains

$$
 N_k=\binom nk(q-1)^k,\qquad V_q(n,t)=\sum_{k=0}^tN_k.
$$
\alttext(A shell at distance k chooses the changed positions and one of q minus one replacement symbols at each. A ball sums shells from zero through its radius.)

Disjoint correction balls around M codewords must fit into the q-to-the-n word space,
so \(M V_q(n,t)\le q^n\), the sphere-packing bound. For the length-three repetition
code, t=1 gives \(2(1+3)=8=2^3\): its two one-error balls partition the cube.
The inequality is a necessary packing constraint, not a general construction of a code.

If a uniformly random independent word is compared with a fixed word, each position
differs with probability \(1-1/q\). Thus its Hamming distance is Binomial(n,1−1/q),
with mean \(n(1-1/q)\) and variance \(n(1-1/q)/q\). For two independent uniform
words the same law holds. This is a model for uniform independent symbols, not a claim
about ordinary language, biological sequences or correlated errors.

The next program computes shell sizes without enumerating every word. For eight bits,
the shell counts are 1,8,28,56,70,56,28,8,1; a radius-two ball contains 37 words.
For eight four-symbol positions, each changed position has three alternatives, shifting
the distance distribution to mean six rather than four. Counts beyond 2^53 in the helper
are approximate floating-point integers; it rejects word-space sizes exceeding double
range. This example remains in a small exactly representable count range.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hamming_shells.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  const auto bits=dm::algorithms::hamming_shells(8),four=dm::algorithms::hamming_shells(8,4);auto probability=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),ball=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  probability.lines(bits.numeric_column("distance").values(),bits.numeric_column("probability").values(),"q = 2",{37,99,235}).lines(four.numeric_column("distance").values(),four.numeric_column("probability").values(),"q = 4",{220,38,38}).title("Uniform words of length eight").x_label("Hamming distance").y_label("probability").y_limits(0,.5);
  ball.lines(bits.numeric_column("distance").values(),bits.numeric_column("cumulative_probability").values(),"q = 2",{37,99,235}).lines(four.numeric_column("distance").values(),four.numeric_column("cumulative_probability").values(),"q = 4",{220,38,38}).title("How much of word space is nearby?").x_label("ball radius").y_label("fraction of all words").y_limits(0,1.3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(probability).add(ball).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/hamming_shells.svg");std::print("Binary alphabet:\n\n{}\n\nFour-symbol alphabet:\n\n{}\n\nRepetition-code radius-one packing: 2 * {:.0f} = 8.\n",bits.to_md(6),four.to_md(6),dm::algorithms::hamming_shells(3).double_at("ball_volume",1));
}
```
