\caption(Exact finite shell counts determine uniform-word distance probabilities and the fraction of word space covered by a ball. Increasing alphabet size increases the typical number of mismatches.)
\alttext(Two probability curves peak near four mismatches for eight binary positions and six for a four-symbol alphabet. A second panel shows cumulative ball fractions; tables give shell and ball counts and probabilities.)


> Conclusion

Hamming distance is a count on an explicitly chosen fixed-width representation. The same
raw byte pair can differ in one byte and eight bits; realignment can reduce edit distance
without changing the positional count. A code's minimum separation determines universal
error guarantees, while nearest-word ties and errors outside the correction radius remain
visible. Shell counting links this geometry to error balls and a binomial law under stated
uniform-independence assumptions. The examples are small finite constructions, not a
forced real-data application or a benchmark of every possible implementation.

> References

- Hamming, R. W. (1950). [Error Detecting and Error Correcting Codes|https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1950.tb00463.x]. ~Bell System Technical Journal~, 29(2), 147–160. Positional distance and error-correction geometry.
- The PGF/TikZ authors. [TikZ and PGF manual|https://tikz.dev/]. Reproducible concept diagrams; this report retains its standalone source and builds a local SVG through LaTeX/dvisvgm.
